-- title:
--   Diagonal of a morphism locally of finite type is locally of finite presentation
-- statement:
--   For schemes $X$ and $Y$ in a fixed universe and a morphism $f : X \to Y$ which is locally of finite type (the typeclass hypothesis `LocallyOfFiniteType f`), the assertion is that the diagonal morphism $\Delta_f : X \to X \times_Y X$, namely Mathlib's `pullback.diagonal f` associated with the pullback of $f$ with itself, is locally of finite presentation in the sense of `LocallyOfFinitePresentation`. No further hypotheses are imposed: finite presentation of $f$ itself is not assumed, only that $f$ be locally of finite type, and no separatedness, quasi-compactness or affineness assumption appears. Since the diagonal of an arbitrary morphism of schemes is an immersion, the content is the finite presentation of the corresponding surjections of rings, i.e. that the relevant kernels are finitely generated.
--
--   This is the standard fact that $\Delta_{X/Y}$ is locally of finite presentation as soon as $f$ is locally of finite type (Stacks 0818, EGA IV 1.4.3.1), the local reason being that the kernel of the multiplication map $B \otimes_A B \to B$ is generated by the elements $x \otimes 1 - 1 \otimes x$ for a finite set of algebra generators $x$. It is used in the construction of Hom- and table schemes, and in the study of fake elliptic curves over quaternionic Shimura curves, where closed immersions given by sections are analysed.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_locallyOfFinitePresentation_diagonal_of_locallyOfFiniteType.lean

import Mathlib

